= Health system and societal costs following osteoarthritis diagnosis in primary care
== OARSI World Congress, Vienna
#smallcaps[Date]\ August 2024

#smallcaps[Citation]\ #eval("Wilson R, Pryymachenko Y, Schofield D, Shrestha R, Darlow B, Dowell T, MacRae J, Abbott JH. Health system and societal costs following osteoarthritis diagnosis in primary care. _Osteoarthritis Cartilage_ 2024;32(S1):S333. doi:~#link(\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.joca.2024.02.490\")[10.1016/j.joca.2024.02.490]", mode: "markup")

=== Abstract
*Purpose (the aim of the study)*
The aim of this study was to estimate the incremental public healthcare
utilisation and costs, incidence of comorbid conditions, and socioeconomic
outcomes following newly diagnosed osteoarthritis (OA) in primary care in
New Zealand (NZ).

*Methods*
This was a matched longitudinal cohort study using routinely collected
administrative data from the NZ Integrated Data Infrastructure (IDI), a
linked research database containing individual-level data from government
administrative datasets, Statistics NZ surveys and data collections, and
non-governmental organisations. Patients with a first diagnosis of hip or
knee OA between 2009 and 2010 were identified from primary care electronic
health records.

We used a propensity score matching approach to match each identified OA
case with controls (patients without OA diagnosis) who were as similar as
possible across a large set of pre-diagnosis variables. Extensive
diagnostic checking was undertaken to assess the balance of covariates
between the matched OA and non-OA cohorts, and the matching model
iteratively improved to achieve optimal matching.

Healthcare utilisation, healthcare costs, incidence of comorbid health
conditions, income and employment, receipt of social welfare benefits, and
mortality were measured in administrative record data over 10-year
follow-up and compared between the OA and matched non-OA cohorts by linear
regression. Sensitivity analyses were undertaken to assess the robustness
of results to modelling assumptions, and analyses of population subgroups
were conducted to assess differential outcomes by OA joint (hip or knee),
ethnicity, sex, and socioeconomic deprivation.

*Results*
We successfully matched 792 patients with newly diagnosed OA (99% of
identified OA cases) with 35 904 controls without OA diagnosis. The average
age at diagnosis was 65 years, and 58% were female. The large majority of
patients (91%) were of New Zealand European ethnicity, and 6% were Māori
(New Zealand’s indigenous population). The distributions of all baseline
covariates were very well balanced between the matched case and control
cohorts (standardised mean difference < 0.1 and variance ratio < 1.25 for
all covariates).

Compared with matched controls, OA patients had significantly higher
healthcare utilisation and healthcare costs over the 10 years following
diagnosis. OA was associated with an average of 0.47 (95% CI: 0.09, 0.84)
additional hospitalisations, 0.26 (95% CI: 0.21, 0.30) additional
publicly-funded joint replacement surgeries, 8.9 (95% CI: 3.7, 14.2)
additional outpatient clinic visits, and 0.32 (95% CI: 0.03, 0.61)
additional emergency department visits; a 9.0 percentage point (95% CI:
6.2, 11.9) increase in the likelihood of opioid prescribing and 6.9
percentage point (95% CI: 4.1, 9.7) increase in NSAID prescribing; and
total excess public healthcare costs of \$9 766 (95% CI: 2 720, 16 812) per
patient. There was no consistent evidence of differences in socioeconomic
outcomes, mortality, or incidence of comorbid conditions.

The results were robust to variation in the selection of baseline
covariates in the regression models, the estimation of generalised linear
models instead of linear regression models, and excluding from the control
group individuals who had a subsequent diagnosis of OA during the follow-up
period.

Subgroup differences in the effects of OA diagnosis were generally
inconclusive, although some suggestive patterns were observed. The
incremental impacts of hip OA diagnosis were larger than those of knee OA
diagnosis for the likelihood of hospitalisation and joint replacement
surgery, the number of emergency department visits, and the incidence of
mental health conditions. The total public healthcare costs associated with
OA diagnosis were smaller for Māori than for non-Māori patients, despite a
higher rate of joint replacement surgery. The effects on utilisation of
hospital services (but not other health services) and on incidence of
cardiovascular disease were larger for men than for women. The rate of
joint replacement surgery was higher for patients from more
socioeconomically deprived areas.

*Conclusions*
This study provides estimates of the excess healthcare utilisation and
costs following a diagnosis of hip or knee OA in primary care. We found
substantial incremental healthcare use and costs attributable to OA
diagnosis; however, there was little evidence of impacts on other health
and economic outcomes.

There was some evidence of inequities in access to care: both Māori and
patients from more socioeconomically deprived areas had higher rates of
publicly-funded joint replacement surgery, while Māori had lower overall
public healthcare costs. These findings may reflect OA diagnosis coming at
a later stage of disease progression for these populations, less access to
early conservative care options, or less access to private insurance
requiring a greater reliance on the public healthcare sector.

